## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical CAPA design skills: data modeling, lifecycle governance, lightweight APIs, and measurable containment strategies in a multi-tenant, multi-region setup.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model tailored to tenants/regions
- Lifecycle state machine with auditable transitions
- Minimal REST API for creating/updating CAPAs with evidence
- Region/tenant-aware metrics to prove containment
- RCA template and canary remediation plan

## Code Example
```javascript
type CAPA = {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  tenants: string[];
  regions: string[];
  evidence: string[];
  batchInfo: {
    schedule: string;
    duration: number;
    resources: string[];
  };
  rootCause: string;
  corrective: string;
  preventive: string;
  owner: string;
  createdAt: Date;
  updatedAt: Date;
};

// Lifecycle states
type CAPAState = 'Identified' | 'Contained' | 'Investigating' | 'Corrected' | 'Verified';

// REST API endpoints
POST /capas
PUT /capas/:id
GET /capas?tenant=:t&region=:r&state=:s
```

## Metrics & Containment
- Tenant/region latency percentiles pre/post containment
- Batch job resource utilization by tenant/region
- Error rates and success rates during remediation
- Canary deployment success metrics by region

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model CAPA data, implement region-aware governance, and handle third-party feed drift with a real-world workflow, including evidence lineage, owners, and canary rollouts.

## Key Concepts

- CAPA data model: incidentTime, vendor, feed, evidenceLinks, lineage, owners, actions, status, region
- Lifecycle: detect -> contain -> triage -> diagnose -> remediate -> verify -> rollback with region gates
- APIs: minimal CRUD (POST /caps, PUT /caps/{id}) including logs/traces and lineage
- Metrics: containment time by region/vendor, recurrence rate, false positives
- RCA & canary: structured root-cause sections; schema validation; provider failover

